      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Critics argue that standardized tests are embedded within the dominant cultural norms of the早早学制, making them inherently biased against non-dominant cultural frameworks and linguistic minorities. When test items rely on specific vocabulary, idioms, or \"middle-class\" values, students from different socioeconomic or ethnic backgrounds are penalized not for a lack of intelligence, but for not accessing the cultural capital required to decode those questions. This phenomenon, often linked to the \"culture of poverty\" hypothesis or Fischhoff's \"middle-class black bias,\" suggests the tests measure assimilation into a specific class culture rather than pure cognitive ability. Consequently, this systemic bias invalidates claims of objectivity, as the very criteria for \"success\" are disconnected from the diverse realities of the population being assessed.",
